//! examples/cayley_dickson_borromean_rerun.sio
//!
//! Corrected re-run of the §4.3 Borromean-vs-unlinked discrimination
//! experiment from docs/papers/main/cayley_dickson_hierarchy_paper_2026-08-13.md,
//! whose original AUROC≈0.50 ("chance") claim was retracted (§9) after an
//! independent re-run of the checked-in `run_borromean_experiment()` found
//! AUROC=0.000 (perfect separation) — traced to a confound: the original
//! `generate_borromean_path` (smooth cos/sin + small noise) and
//! `generate_unlinked_path` (pure i.i.d. N(0,1)) differ ~10x in local
//! roughness, which by itself is enough to separate the two classes,
//! independent of any linking/associativity structure.
//!
//! THE FIX: both classes are now smooth curves of the SAME functional
//! family and SAME noise level (matched local roughness, verified below
//! by measuring mean |successive difference| for both classes). The only
//! difference is the phase relationship between the three components:
//!
//!   Positive (Borromean-type): x=cos(t), y=sin(t), z=cos(2t)sin(t)
//!     — the frequency-doubled cross term in z couples all three axes.
//!   Negative (unlinked-type): x=cos(t+phi1), y=cos(t+phi2), z=cos(t+phi3)
//!     — three independent-phase copies of the SAME single-frequency
//!       cosine, i.e. structurally decoupled axes (no cross term), which
//!       is the "unlinked" analogue at matched smoothness.
//!
//! Reuses this repo's already-verified [2,1]-hook and sedenion associator
//! (see examples/cayley_dickson_hook21_g2_verification.sio) as the probe
//! features, and reports AUROC + Cohen's d honestly — this file does NOT
//! assume the result will replicate "chance"; it reports whatever comes
//! out, per CLAUDE.md's FAIL_HONEST principle.
//!
//! THREE FURTHER DEFECTS SURFACED WHILE BUILDING THIS RE-RUN (none were in
//! either prior audit) — see the in-code comments at pack_sed() and the
//! printed [Interpretation] block for detail:
//!   1. The original `pack(x)` (s[:3]=x, s[3]=1.0) confines every operand
//!      to sedenion slots {0,1,2,3} — EXACTLY the quaternion subalgebra H
//!      under Cayley-Dickson doubling, which is associative. Associator
//!      (and hence [2,1]-hook) was IDENTICALLY ZERO by a theorem, for
//!      BOTH classes, regardless of roughness or linking. Verified here:
//!      ||associator|| ~ 1e-9 (machine epsilon) on 3 random such vectors.
//!   2. Fixing (1) by spreading coordinates across non-quaternion slots
//!      makes the ordinary associator genuinely nonzero — but the
//!      [2,1]-hook STILL reads exactly 0, because packing a,b,c as scalar
//!      amplitudes along the SAME FIXED 3 imaginary directions (however
//!      the coefficients are assigned to those 3 slots — verified with a
//!      cyclic rotation AND a fully i.i.d. random permutation, byte-
//!      identical zero both times) makes every associator a pure-basis-
//!      triple associator, which is a textbook fact ALWAYS totally
//!      antisymmetric (Lambda^3) — independent of coefficients, of the
//!      assignment, or of Borromean-vs-unlinked structure.
//!   3. Escaping (2) requires a, b, c to collectively span >=4 independent
//!      imaginary directions, not 3. Adding one genuinely independent 4th
//!      feature per operand (a one-step delay-embedded coordinate) into a
//!      4th slot finally makes BOTH the associator and the [2,1]-hook
//!      nonzero. At n=15/class this looked like a real effect (hook
//!      AUROC=0.25); at n=25/class it moved MUCH closer to chance (hook
//!      AUROC=0.32) — the direction a small-sample artifact would move.
//!
//! RESULT (2026-08-15, n=50/class, K_PERMS=2000 label reshuffles, seed
//! 20260814 -- reproduced byte-for-byte across repeat runs): hook
//! AUROC=0.392 (Cohen's d=-0.42, permutation p=0.060) and associator
//! AUROC=0.416 (d=-0.25, p=0.154). NEITHER survives an uncorrected
//! p<0.05 threshold (hook is borderline at p=0.06). DO NOT read the
//! AUROC series (n=15: 0.25 -> n=25: 0.32 -> n=50: 0.39, hook) as
//! "shrinking toward chance" -- a math-review pass caught an earlier
//! draft of this docstring making exactly that claim as an over-read:
//! Cohen's d has been essentially flat since n=25 (-0.44 -> -0.42, not
//! still shrinking). At n=50 specifically, AUROC ~= Phi(d/sqrt(2))
//! applied to that stable d predicts ~=0.38, close to the observed 0.39
//! -- a single-point match, NOT validation of the whole n=15->25->50
//! trajectory: at n=15/25, applying the same link to that n's (still-
//! moving) d gives a HIGHER predicted AUROC than what was actually
//! observed (predicted~=0.31 vs observed 0.25 at n=15; ~=0.38 vs 0.32 at
//! n=25) -- the link overpredicts AUROC at small n, the opposite of what
//! a naive "small-n AUROC biased toward 0.5" story needs to hold across
//! the whole series. That's further reason not to over-read the
//! trajectory in EITHER direction, not support for the stable-effect
//! reading beyond the single n=50 match above.
//! HONEST BOTTOM LINE: with every identified confound and structural
//! degeneracy fixed AND a real permutation-test p-value computed for the
//! first time, this experiment FAILS TO REJECT the null at alpha=0.05 --
//! which is not the same as CONFIRMING the null (p=0.06 is compatible
//! with both a real small effect and no effect at all; no CI, power
//! curve, or equivalence test was computed to tell them apart). What
//! changed is that there is now a real, if inconclusive, statistical
//! test in place of "underpowered to say anything." n=75/class was ALSO attempted and hit
//! Madaros's documented resource ceiling (exit 182, the same one already
//! hit at 4 seeds in examples/cayley_dickson_octtree_realtree_training.sio
//! and examples/cayley_dickson_sedentree_realtree16_training.sio); n=50
//! is the largest verified-complete scale for this file's current
//! structure. Treat the original paper's numbers, the initial flat
//! retraction, and this file's own n=15/n=25 (no p-value) intermediate
//! results as all superseded by the numbers above. See the paper's §9
//! for how this is now reported.
//!
//! Run: SOUNIO_STDLIB_PATH=$(pwd)/stdlib ./bin/souc run \
//!        examples/cayley_dickson_borromean_rerun.sio
//!      (this file needs no extern "C" FFI -- unlike the LEMON pilot,
//!      Madaros, the default engine, is the right tool here. It was
//!      checked against SOUNIO_SOUC_ENGINE=lean_single too: that engine's
//!      effect checker rejects this file's sin_f64/sqrt_f64 for reasons
//!      that did not reproduce under direct inspection or under Madaros's
//!      own checker (`souc check` reports clean) -- not investigated
//!      further since Madaros already runs this file correctly.)

const PATH_T: i64 = 100
const N_SAMPLES: i64 = 50   // per class
const MAXN: i64 = 256       // >= 2*N_SAMPLES, scratch array size for AUROC

// =============================================================================
// PRINT HELPERS
// =============================================================================

fn print_i64(x: i64) with IO, Mut, Div, Panic {
    if x < 0 { print("-"); print_i64(0 - x) }
    else if x < 10 { print_digit(x) }
    else { print_i64(x / 10); print_digit(x - (x / 10) * 10) }
}

fn print_digit(d: i64) with IO {
    if d == 0 { print("0") } else if d == 1 { print("1") }
    else if d == 2 { print("2") } else if d == 3 { print("3") }
    else if d == 4 { print("4") } else if d == 5 { print("5") }
    else if d == 6 { print("6") } else if d == 7 { print("7") }
    else if d == 8 { print("8") } else { print("9") }
}

fn print_f64(x: f64, decimals: i64) with IO, Mut, Div, Panic {
    if x < 0.0 { print("-"); print_f64(0.0 - x, decimals) }
    else {
        let int_part = x as i64
        print_i64(int_part)
        print(".")
        var frac = x - (int_part as f64)
        var d: i64 = 0
        while d < decimals {
            frac = frac * 10.0
            let digit = frac as i64
            print_digit(digit)
            frac = frac - (digit as f64)
            d = d + 1
        }
    }
}

// =============================================================================
// PRNG + TRIG (self-contained; sin/cos mirror stdlib/nn/autograd.sio's
// Taylor-with-range-reduction implementation)
// =============================================================================

var RNG_STATE: [i64; 1] = [0; 1]
fn rng_seed(seed: i64) with Mut { RNG_STATE[0] = seed }
fn rng_next_u64() -> i64 with Mut {
    RNG_STATE[0] = RNG_STATE[0] + 0x9e3779b97f4a7c15 as i64
    var z = RNG_STATE[0]
    z = z ^ (z >> 30)
    z = z * 0xbf58476d as i64
    z = z ^ (z >> 27)
    z = z * 0x94d049bb as i64
    z = z ^ (z >> 31)
    z
}
fn rng_next_signed() -> f64 with Mut, Div {
    let u = rng_next_u64() & 0x7fffffff
    let v = (u as f64) / 1073741823.5
    v - 1.0
}
fn rng_next_unit() -> f64 with Mut, Div {
    (rng_next_signed() + 1.0) / 2.0  // uniform [0,1]
}

fn sin_f64(x: f64) -> f64 with Div {
    var v = x
    while v > 3.14159265358979 { v = v - 6.28318530717959 }
    while v < 0.0 - 3.14159265358979 { v = v + 6.28318530717959 }
    let x2 = v * v
    var term = v
    var sum = v
    term = 0.0 - term * x2 / 6.0;   sum = sum + term
    term = 0.0 - term * x2 / 20.0;  sum = sum + term
    term = 0.0 - term * x2 / 42.0;  sum = sum + term
    term = 0.0 - term * x2 / 72.0;  sum = sum + term
    term = 0.0 - term * x2 / 110.0; sum = sum + term
    sum
}
fn cos_f64(x: f64) -> f64 with Div { sin_f64(x + 1.5707963267949) }

fn sqrt_f64(x: f64) -> f64 with Div, Panic {
    if x <= 0.0 { return 0.0 }
    var g = x
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < 30 { g = (g + x/g) / 2.0; i = i + 1 }
    g
}

// =============================================================================
// SEDENION PRODUCT (identical to examples/cayley_dickson_hook21_g2_verification.sio,
// copied for self-containment)
// =============================================================================

fn oct_mul(a: &[f64; 8], b: &[f64; 8], out: &![f64; 8]) with Mut, Div, NonAssoc {
    var r0 = a[0]*b[0] - a[1]*b[1] - a[2]*b[2] - a[3]*b[3] - a[4]*b[4] - a[5]*b[5] - a[6]*b[6] - a[7]*b[7]
    out[0] = r0
    var r1 = a[0]*b[1] + a[1]*b[0] + a[2]*b[3] - a[3]*b[2] + a[4]*b[5] - a[5]*b[4] - a[6]*b[7] + a[7]*b[6]
    out[1] = r1
    var r2 = a[0]*b[2] + a[2]*b[0] - a[1]*b[3] + a[3]*b[1] + a[4]*b[6] - a[6]*b[4] + a[5]*b[7] - a[7]*b[5]
    out[2] = r2
    var r3 = a[0]*b[3] + a[3]*b[0] + a[1]*b[2] - a[2]*b[1] + a[4]*b[7] - a[7]*b[4] - a[5]*b[6] + a[6]*b[5]
    out[3] = r3
    var r4 = a[0]*b[4] + a[4]*b[0] - a[1]*b[5] + a[5]*b[1] - a[2]*b[6] + a[6]*b[2] - a[3]*b[7] + a[7]*b[3]
    out[4] = r4
    var r5 = a[0]*b[5] + a[5]*b[0] + a[1]*b[4] - a[4]*b[1] - a[2]*b[7] + a[7]*b[2] + a[3]*b[6] - a[6]*b[3]
    out[5] = r5
    var r6 = a[0]*b[6] + a[6]*b[0] + a[1]*b[7] - a[7]*b[1] + a[2]*b[4] - a[4]*b[2] - a[3]*b[5] + a[5]*b[3]
    out[6] = r6
    var r7 = a[0]*b[7] + a[7]*b[0] - a[1]*b[6] + a[6]*b[1] + a[2]*b[5] - a[5]*b[2] + a[3]*b[4] - a[4]*b[3]
    out[7] = r7
}

fn sed_conj(src: &[f64; 8], dst: &![f64; 8]) with Mut {
    dst[0] = src[0]
    var i: i64 = 1
    while i < 8 { dst[i] = 0.0 - src[i]; i = i + 1 }
}

var SED_P: [f64; 8] = [0.0; 8]
var SED_Q: [f64; 8] = [0.0; 8]

fn sed_mul(a: &[f64; 16], b: &[f64; 16], out: &![f64; 16]) with Mut, Div, NonAssoc {
    var a_lo: [f64; 8] = [0.0; 8]
    var a_hi: [f64; 8] = [0.0; 8]
    var b_lo: [f64; 8] = [0.0; 8]
    var b_hi: [f64; 8] = [0.0; 8]
    var conj: [f64; 8] = [0.0; 8]
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < 8 {
        a_lo[i] = a[i]; a_hi[i] = a[8 + i]
        b_lo[i] = b[i]; b_hi[i] = b[8 + i]
        i = i + 1
    }
    oct_mul(&a_lo, &b_lo, &!SED_P)
    sed_conj(&b_hi, &!conj)
    oct_mul(&conj, &a_hi, &!SED_Q)
    i = 0
    while i < 8 { out[i] = SED_P[i] - SED_Q[i]; i = i + 1 }
    oct_mul(&b_hi, &a_lo, &!SED_P)
    sed_conj(&b_lo, &!conj)
    oct_mul(&a_hi, &conj, &!SED_Q)
    i = 0
    while i < 8 { out[8 + i] = SED_P[i] + SED_Q[i]; i = i + 1 }
}

fn sed_sub(a: &[f64; 16], b: &[f64; 16], out: &![f64; 16]) with Mut {
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < 16 { out[i] = a[i] - b[i]; i = i + 1 }
}

fn sed_norm(a: &[f64; 16]) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var s = 0.0; var i: i64 = 0
    while i < 16 { s = s + a[i]*a[i]; i = i + 1 }
    sqrt_f64(s)
}

fn sed_associator(a: &[f64; 16], b: &[f64; 16], c: &[f64; 16], out: &![f64; 16]) with Mut, Div, NonAssoc {
    var ab: [f64; 16] = [0.0; 16]
    sed_mul(a, b, &!ab)
    var ab_c: [f64; 16] = [0.0; 16]
    sed_mul(&ab, c, &!ab_c)
    var bc: [f64; 16] = [0.0; 16]
    sed_mul(b, c, &!bc)
    var a_bc: [f64; 16] = [0.0; 16]
    sed_mul(a, &bc, &!a_bc)
    sed_sub(&ab_c, &a_bc, out)
}

// [2,1]-hook, closed form (2T(a,b,c) - T(b,c,a) - T(c,a,b))/3 — see
// examples/cayley_dickson_hook21_g2_verification.sio for the derivation.
fn hook21_sed_closed(a: &[f64; 16], b: &[f64; 16], c: &[f64; 16], out: &![f64; 16]) with Mut, Div, NonAssoc {
    var tabc: [f64; 16] = [0.0; 16]
    var tbca: [f64; 16] = [0.0; 16]
    var tcab: [f64; 16] = [0.0; 16]
    sed_associator(a, b, c, &!tabc)
    sed_associator(b, c, a, &!tbca)
    sed_associator(c, a, b, &!tcab)
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < 16 {
        out[i] = (2.0 * tabc[i] - tbca[i] - tcab[i]) / 3.0
        i = i + 1
    }
}

// =============================================================================
// PATH GENERATORS (matched local roughness — see file header)
// =============================================================================

var PATH_X: [f64; 100] = [0.0; 100]
var PATH_Y: [f64; 100] = [0.0; 100]
var PATH_Z: [f64; 100] = [0.0; 100]
// One of 6 permutations of (SLOT_A,SLOT_B,SLOT_C), i.i.d. per time step —
// used to test whether the cyclic (deterministic) role rotation's
// continued hook21=0 was itself a hidden symmetry of that SPECIFIC
// regular rotation, or a more general property of sparse 3-slot packing.
var PATH_ROLE: [i64; 100] = [0; 100]

fn randomize_roles() with Mut, Div {
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < PATH_T {
        PATH_ROLE[i as usize] = rng_nonneg(6)
        i = i + 1
    }
}

fn rng_nonneg(bound: i64) -> i64 with Mut, Div {
    let u = rng_next_u64() & 0x7fffffff
    u - (u / bound) * bound
}

fn generate_borromean_path(noise_sigma: f64) with Mut, Div {
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < PATH_T {
        let t = 12.56637061435917 * (i as f64) / ((PATH_T - 1) as f64)  // 0..4*pi
        PATH_X[i as usize] = cos_f64(t) + noise_sigma * rng_next_signed()
        PATH_Y[i as usize] = sin_f64(t) + noise_sigma * rng_next_signed()
        PATH_Z[i as usize] = cos_f64(2.0 * t) * sin_f64(t) + noise_sigma * rng_next_signed()
        i = i + 1
    }
    randomize_roles()
}

fn generate_unlinked_path(noise_sigma: f64) with Mut, Div {
    let phi1 = 6.28318530717959 * rng_next_unit()
    let phi2 = 6.28318530717959 * rng_next_unit()
    let phi3 = 6.28318530717959 * rng_next_unit()
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < PATH_T {
        let t = 12.56637061435917 * (i as f64) / ((PATH_T - 1) as f64)
        PATH_X[i as usize] = cos_f64(t + phi1) + noise_sigma * rng_next_signed()
        PATH_Y[i as usize] = cos_f64(t + phi2) + noise_sigma * rng_next_signed()
        PATH_Z[i as usize] = cos_f64(t + phi3) + noise_sigma * rng_next_signed()
        i = i + 1
    }
    randomize_roles()
}

// Mean |successive difference| across all 3 axes — the roughness probe
// that caught the original confound (0.138 vs 1.435).
fn path_roughness() -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var sum = 0.0
    var i: i64 = 1
    while i < PATH_T {
        let dx = PATH_X[i as usize] - PATH_X[(i-1) as usize]
        let dy = PATH_Y[i as usize] - PATH_Y[(i-1) as usize]
        let dz = PATH_Z[i as usize] - PATH_Z[(i-1) as usize]
        let adx = if dx < 0.0 { 0.0 - dx } else { dx }
        let ady = if dy < 0.0 { 0.0 - dy } else { dy }
        let adz = if dz < 0.0 { 0.0 - dz } else { dz }
        sum = sum + adx + ady + adz
        i = i + 1
    }
    sum / ((PATH_T - 1) as f64 * 3.0)
}

// FIXED (2026-08-14, second correction): the original packing put
// (x,y,z,bias) into sedenion slots {0,1,2,3}. Under Cayley-Dickson
// doubling H -> O -> S, those 4 slots are EXACTLY the quaternion
// subalgebra H, which is associative — so every triple's associator
// (and hence the [2,1]-hook) was IDENTICALLY ZERO by a theorem,
// regardless of Borromean vs unlinked structure (verified above,
// ||associator||~1e-9 = machine epsilon, on 3 random such vectors).
//
// FIXED AGAIN (2026-08-14, third correction): merely moving off the
// quaternion subalgebra was not enough. As long as a=pack(t-2),
// b=pack(t-1), c=pack(t) all use the SAME FIXED assignment of
// (x,y,z) -> (slot_i,slot_j,slot_k), every associator reduces to a
// scalar multiple of the single pure-basis-triple associator
// [e_i,e_j,e_k], which is a general algebraic fact ALWAYS antisymmetric
// under permutation (lies entirely in Lambda^3) — so hook21 = 0 no
// matter which 3 slots are chosen (verified identical to 8dp across
// two unrelated slot choices).
//
// FIXED A THIRD TIME (2026-08-14): neither the cyclic rotation nor a
// fully i.i.d.-random permutation of roles broke the zero — both still
// gave hook21=0.00000000 to 8dp. Root cause, now understood cleanly:
// a, b, c stayed confined to the SAME 4-dimensional span{e0,e_i,e_j,e_k}
// (bias + exactly 3 fixed imaginary directions {SLOT_A,SLOT_B,SLOT_C})
// in EVERY variant — only which coefficient landed in which of those 3
// slots changed, never the SPAN itself. This is the general fact: for
// any three vectors confined to a fixed span of the real unit plus
// EXACTLY 3 imaginary basis directions, the associator is a multilinear
// combination of permutations of the single basis-triple associator
// [e_i,e_j,e_k] — which is a textbook fact ALWAYS totally antisymmetric
// under permutation of 3 DISTINCT basis units — so it always lands
// entirely in Lambda^3 and [2,1] vanishes, independent of coefficients
// or their assignment. Escaping this requires a, b, c to collectively
// engage >=4 independent imaginary directions, not just 3.
//
// THE FIX: pack 4 independent physical quantities per operand — the 3
// path coordinates PLUS a genuinely independent 4th (the previous
// z-value, a short delay-embedding feature) — into 4 distinct slots
// {SLOT_A,SLOT_B,SLOT_C,SLOT_D}, breaking the 3-direction confinement.
const SLOT_A: i64 = 1    // quaternion-imaginary
const SLOT_B: i64 = 5    // octonion non-quaternion
const SLOT_C: i64 = 9    // sedenion "hi" half
const SLOT_D: i64 = 11   // sedenion "hi" half, 2nd independent direction

fn pack_sed(t: i64, out: &![f64; 16]) with Mut, Div {
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < 16 { out[i] = 0.0; i = i + 1 }
    out[0] = 1.0                       // bias / real part
    let x = PATH_X[t as usize]
    let y = PATH_Y[t as usize]
    let z = PATH_Z[t as usize]
    let w = if t > 0 { PATH_Z[(t - 1) as usize] } else { 0.0 }  // 4th, independent (delay-embedded) feature
    out[SLOT_A as usize] = x
    out[SLOT_B as usize] = y
    out[SLOT_C as usize] = z
    out[SLOT_D as usize] = w
}

// Mean hook-norm and mean associator-norm over consecutive triples of the
// CURRENT path in PATH_X/Y/Z (out[0]=hook_mean, out[1]=assoc_mean).
fn compute_hook_features(out: &![f64; 2]) with Mut, Div, Panic, NonAssoc {
    var hook_sum = 0.0
    var assoc_sum = 0.0
    var count: i64 = 0
    var t: i64 = 2
    while t < PATH_T {
        var a: [f64; 16] = [0.0; 16]
        var b: [f64; 16] = [0.0; 16]
        var c: [f64; 16] = [0.0; 16]
        pack_sed(t - 2, &!a)
        pack_sed(t - 1, &!b)
        pack_sed(t, &!c)
        var hook: [f64; 16] = [0.0; 16]
        hook21_sed_closed(&a, &b, &c, &!hook)
        hook_sum = hook_sum + sed_norm(&hook)
        var assoc: [f64; 16] = [0.0; 16]
        sed_associator(&a, &b, &c, &!assoc)
        assoc_sum = assoc_sum + sed_norm(&assoc)
        count = count + 1
        t = t + 1
    }
    out[0] = hook_sum / (count as f64)
    out[1] = assoc_sum / (count as f64)
}

// =============================================================================
// STATS: AUROC (Mann-Whitney rank formula) + Cohen's d
// =============================================================================

fn auroc(scores: &[f64; 256], labels: &[i64; 256], n: i64, n_pos: i64, n_neg: i64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var rank_sum_pos = 0.0
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < n {
        if labels[i as usize] == 1 {
            var rank = 1.0
            var j: i64 = 0
            while j < n {
                if j != i && scores[j as usize] < scores[i as usize] { rank = rank + 1.0 }
                j = j + 1
            }
            rank_sum_pos = rank_sum_pos + rank
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    let npf = n_pos as f64
    (rank_sum_pos - npf * (npf + 1.0) / 2.0) / (npf * (n_neg as f64))
}

fn mean_of(scores: &[f64; 256], labels: &[i64; 256], n: i64, target_label: i64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var s = 0.0; var c: i64 = 0
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < n {
        if labels[i as usize] == target_label { s = s + scores[i as usize]; c = c + 1 }
        i = i + 1
    }
    s / (c as f64)
}

fn std_of(scores: &[f64; 256], labels: &[i64; 256], n: i64, target_label: i64, mean: f64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    var s = 0.0; var c: i64 = 0
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < n {
        if labels[i as usize] == target_label {
            let d = scores[i as usize] - mean
            s = s + d*d; c = c + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    sqrt_f64(s / (c as f64))
}

fn cohens_d(scores: &[f64; 256], labels: &[i64; 256], n: i64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    let m1 = mean_of(scores, labels, n, 1)
    let m0 = mean_of(scores, labels, n, 0)
    let s1 = std_of(scores, labels, n, 1, m1)
    let s0 = std_of(scores, labels, n, 0, m0)
    (m1 - m0) / sqrt_f64((s1*s1 + s0*s0) / 2.0)
}

// =============================================================================
// PERMUTATION TEST (Fisher-Yates reshuffle of the label/class assignment,
// AUROC recomputed each draw, two-sided on |AUROC-0.5|): the real
// significance test this file's own trailing note has flagged as missing
// since n=15/class. Scores are the already-computed, expensive-to-generate
// per-sample features; only the label assignment is reshuffled per draw,
// so K_PERMS draws cost K_PERMS AUROC evaluations (O(n^2) each), not
// K_PERMS path regenerations.
// =============================================================================

const K_PERMS: i64 = 2000

fn permutation_p_value(scores: &[f64; 256], n: i64, n_pos: i64, n_neg: i64, observed_auc: f64) -> f64 with Mut, Div, Panic {
    let observed_dev = if observed_auc > 0.5 { observed_auc - 0.5 } else { 0.5 - observed_auc }
    var perm_labels: [i64; 256] = [0; 256]
    var count_extreme: i64 = 0
    var p: i64 = 0
    while p < K_PERMS {
        var i: i64 = 0
        while i < n { perm_labels[i as usize] = if i < n_pos { 1 } else { 0 }; i = i + 1 }
        var j: i64 = n - 1
        while j > 0 {
            let r = rng_nonneg(j + 1)
            let tmp = perm_labels[j as usize]
            perm_labels[j as usize] = perm_labels[r as usize]
            perm_labels[r as usize] = tmp
            j = j - 1
        }
        let perm_auc = auroc(scores, &perm_labels, n, n_pos, n_neg)
        let perm_dev = if perm_auc > 0.5 { perm_auc - 0.5 } else { 0.5 - perm_auc }
        if perm_dev >= observed_dev { count_extreme = count_extreme + 1 }
        p = p + 1
    }
    (count_extreme as f64) / (K_PERMS as f64)
}

// =============================================================================
// MAIN
// =============================================================================

fn main() -> i32 with IO, Mut, Div, Panic, NonAssoc {
    print("=== Borromean-vs-unlinked re-run, matched local roughness ===\n")
    rng_seed(20260814)
    let noise_sigma = 0.1

    // --- Hypothesis check: pack_sed puts (x,y,z,bias) into sedenion slots
    //     0..3. Under the standard Cayley-Dickson doubling H -> O -> S,
    //     slots {0,1,2,3} are EXACTLY the quaternion subalgebra H (the
    //     "lower half of the lower half"), which is associative. If true,
    //     ANY triple confined to those 4 slots has associator IDENTICALLY
    //     ZERO — a theorem, not a measurement — independent of Borromean
    //     vs unlinked structure. Verify on 3 random such vectors before
    //     trusting the AUROC below.
    var qa: [f64; 16] = [0.0; 16]
    var qb: [f64; 16] = [0.0; 16]
    var qc: [f64; 16] = [0.0; 16]
    var qi: i64 = 0
    while qi < 4 {
        qa[qi as usize] = rng_next_signed()
        qb[qi as usize] = rng_next_signed()
        qc[qi as usize] = rng_next_signed()
        qi = qi + 1
    }
    var qassoc: [f64; 16] = [0.0; 16]
    sed_associator(&qa, &qb, &qc, &!qassoc)
    print("\n[Hypothesis check] associator of 3 random vectors confined to\n")
    print("  sedenion slots {0,1,2,3} (the quaternion subalgebra):\n")
    print("  ||associator|| = "); print_f64(sed_norm(&qassoc), 12)
    print("  (should be EXACTLY 0 if the pack() embedding never leaves H)\n")

    // --- Roughness sanity check (this is the exact probe that caught
    //     the original confound; both classes should now be close) ---
    generate_borromean_path(noise_sigma)
    let rough_pos = path_roughness()
    generate_unlinked_path(noise_sigma)
    let rough_neg = path_roughness()
    print("\n[Sanity] mean |successive difference| (local roughness):\n")
    print("  Borromean-type: "); print_f64(rough_pos, 4); print("\n")
    print("  unlinked-type:  "); print_f64(rough_neg, 4); print("\n")
    print("  (original confound was 0.138 vs 1.435, ~10x apart; these should be close)\n")

    // --- Generate N_SAMPLES per class, collect hook_mean/assoc_mean ---
    var hook_scores: [f64; 256] = [0.0; 256]
    var assoc_scores: [f64; 256] = [0.0; 256]
    var labels: [i64; 256] = [0; 256]
    var idx: i64 = 0

    var s: i64 = 0
    while s < N_SAMPLES {
        generate_borromean_path(noise_sigma)
        var feat: [f64; 2] = [0.0; 2]
        compute_hook_features(&!feat)
        hook_scores[idx as usize] = feat[0]
        assoc_scores[idx as usize] = feat[1]
        labels[idx as usize] = 1
        idx = idx + 1
        s = s + 1
    }
    s = 0
    while s < N_SAMPLES {
        generate_unlinked_path(noise_sigma)
        var feat: [f64; 2] = [0.0; 2]
        compute_hook_features(&!feat)
        hook_scores[idx as usize] = feat[0]
        assoc_scores[idx as usize] = feat[1]
        labels[idx as usize] = 0
        idx = idx + 1
        s = s + 1
    }
    let n_total = idx

    print("\n[Results] n="); print_i64(N_SAMPLES); print(" per class, roughness-matched:\n")

    let hook_m1 = mean_of(&hook_scores, &labels, n_total, 1)
    let hook_m0 = mean_of(&hook_scores, &labels, n_total, 0)
    let hook_auc = auroc(&hook_scores, &labels, n_total, N_SAMPLES, N_SAMPLES)
    let hook_d = cohens_d(&hook_scores, &labels, n_total)
    print("  [2,1]-hook:   Borromean mean="); print_f64(hook_m1, 8)
    print("  unlinked mean="); print_f64(hook_m0, 8)
    print("  AUROC="); print_f64(hook_auc, 4)
    print("  Cohen's d="); print_f64(hook_d, 4); print("\n")

    let assoc_m1 = mean_of(&assoc_scores, &labels, n_total, 1)
    let assoc_m0 = mean_of(&assoc_scores, &labels, n_total, 0)
    let assoc_auc = auroc(&assoc_scores, &labels, n_total, N_SAMPLES, N_SAMPLES)
    let assoc_d = cohens_d(&assoc_scores, &labels, n_total)
    print("  associator:   Borromean mean="); print_f64(assoc_m1, 8)
    print("  unlinked mean="); print_f64(assoc_m0, 8)
    print("  AUROC="); print_f64(assoc_auc, 4)
    print("  Cohen's d="); print_f64(assoc_d, 4); print("\n")

    print("\n[Permutation test] "); print_i64(K_PERMS); print(" label reshuffles,\n")
    print(" two-sided on |AUROC-0.5| (the real significance test this file's own\n")
    print(" prior version flagged as missing -- p = fraction of reshuffled label\n")
    print(" assignments whose |AUROC-0.5| is at least as large as the observed one):\n")
    let hook_p = permutation_p_value(&hook_scores, n_total, N_SAMPLES, N_SAMPLES, hook_auc)
    let assoc_p = permutation_p_value(&assoc_scores, n_total, N_SAMPLES, N_SAMPLES, assoc_auc)
    print("  [2,1]-hook:   p="); print_f64(hook_p, 4); print("\n")
    print("  associator:   p="); print_f64(assoc_p, 4); print("\n")

    print("\n[Interpretation, honest, final state]:\n")
    print(" Four defects fixed in sequence: (1) roughness confound, (2) operands\n")
    print(" confined to the associative quaternion subalgebra (hook=assoc=0 by a\n")
    print(" theorem, verified), (3) operands confined to a fixed 3-imaginary-\n")
    print(" direction span (hook=0 for ANY coefficients/permutation within that\n")
    print(" span, also a general fact, verified with 3 unrelated slot/role\n")
    print(" schemes), (4) escaping (3) required a genuinely 4th independent\n")
    print(" basis direction per operand (here: a one-step delay-embedded\n")
    print(" feature). With that: [2,1]-hook and associator are BOTH now\n")
    print(" genuinely nonzero for the first time in this investigation.\n")
    print(" DO NOT read the AUROC series (n=15: 0.25/0.31, n=25: 0.32/0.34,\n")
    print(" n=50: 0.39/0.42, hook/associator) as \"shrinking toward chance\" --\n")
    print(" Cohen's d for the hook has been essentially flat since n=25\n")
    print(" (-0.44 -> -0.42, not still shrinking). At n=50 specifically,\n")
    print(" AUROC ~= Phi(d/sqrt(2)) applied to that stable d predicts ~0.38,\n")
    print(" close to the observed 0.39 -- a SINGLE-POINT match, not\n")
    print(" validation of the whole n=15->25->50 trajectory: at n=15/25 the\n")
    print(" same link (applied to that n's still-moving d) predicts a HIGHER\n")
    print(" AUROC than observed (~0.31 vs 0.25 at n=15; ~0.38 vs 0.32 at\n")
    print(" n=25) -- overpredicting, the opposite of what \"small-n AUROC\n")
    print(" biased toward 0.5\" needs across the whole series.\n")
    print(" That's enough to make \"the effect is decaying to zero\" an\n")
    print(" unearned reading, without establishing the reverse (a stable\n")
    print(" small effect) either. Read the printed p-values above as a\n")
    print(" genuine hypothesis\n")
    print(" test, not as proof either way: p<0.05 (two-sided, uncorrected\n")
    print(" across the 2 features tested here) would mean the AUROCs above are\n")
    print(" unlikely under the null; p>=0.05 (what was actually observed, hook\n")
    print(" p=0.06 borderline) means this run FAILS TO REJECT the null -- which\n")
    print(" is not the same as CONFIRMING it. No CI, power curve, or\n")
    print(" equivalence test (TOST) was computed to adjudicate between \"no\n")
    print(" effect\" and \"stable small effect,\" and the resource ceiling that\n")
    print(" blocked n=75/class is exactly what would be needed to try. What IS\n")
    print(" true: this is now a real, if inconclusive, statistical test, not a\n")
    print(" provisional \"not yet powered to test at all\" state -- the original\n")
    print(" paper's AUROC numbers, the flat retraction, and this file's own\n")
    print(" earlier (n=15/n=25, no p-value) intermediate results are all\n")
    print(" superseded by the numbers above, but so is any \"resolved to\n")
    print(" chance\" reading of them.\n")

    print("\n=== DONE ===\n")
    0
}
